Using generic types.

Before writing your own generics, get fluent at reading and instantiating the ones the library already gives you. Most day-to-day generics work is exactly this: supplying type arguments to an existing generic type, and letting the compiler check the rest.

A generic type declares its parameters in angle brackets, and you use it by putting concrete types there. A list of string, where E is string. A map from long to user, where K is long and V is user. An optional of big-decimal, where T is big-decimal. Those angle-bracket parts are the type arguments. Once supplied, every method specialises: add demands a string, get returns a user, or-else takes a big-decimal. The compiler substitutes and checks, and you never cast.

Now, the diamond, which saves you from repeating yourself. You must state the type argument on the variable, on the left. But on the new side, the compiler can infer it from context, and the empty angle brackets are the diamond operator. So you write a map from string to list of integer, equals new hash-map, with an empty diamond — not the full type spelled out again. The diamond says "same type arguments as the left-hand side", which keeps a nested generic readable. One twist: with var, the type instead comes from the right, so you put the angle-string on the right — var names equals new array-list of string.

Type arguments compose freely, because a type argument can itself be generic. A map from string to a list of order. A list of maps from string to integer. A map from region to a map from string to long. You read these inside-out — a map from string to list of order is "a map from string, to a list of order". And when they get deeply nested, that's a hint to introduce a record, or a small wrapper, for readability — though the compiler will happily handle any depth.

Library signatures use type parameters too, and learning to read them pays off immediately. On a map, get-or-default returns a V. On list-of, the signature has a leading angle-T, and returns a list of T, with T inferred from the arguments. And optional's map method takes a function with some question-mark-extends and question-mark-super in it — which we'll decode later. The V and T are placeholders, bound either by the enclosing type or by the call. And a leading angle-T, as in list-of, means the method introduces its own parameter — which is the subject of section four.

Finally, the one thing to avoid: raw types. Using a generic type with no type argument at all gives you a raw type — a backward-compatibility relic that switches type-checking off. Write a raw list, and the compiler warns "unchecked". You can add "ok", and then add forty-two, with no error at all — you've lost all your safety. So never write raw types in new code; they exist only so that code from before two thousand four still compiles. If you genuinely mean "a list of anything", the right spelling is the unbounded wildcard, list of question-mark, from section six, which stays type-safe. With reading and instantiating in hand, the next step is writing a type that has a parameter of its own.
